## Task Summary
Select best candidate for long-distance pilot.

## Candidates – Key Evidence (as recorded)

- Candidate A: For: anticipates dangerous situations; sees complex connections; excellent spatial vision; very good leadership qualities. Against: regarded as show‑off; not open to new ideas.

- Candidate B: For: very conscientious; handles stress very well (agent_3); good at assessing weather conditions; excellent computer skills. Against: relatively weak memory for numbers; makes nasty remarks about colleagues.

- Candidate C: For: makes correct decisions quickly; keeps calm in a crisis; understands complicated technology; 100 % reliable (agent_1). Against: difficulty communicating ideas (agent_2); regarded as egocentric; not very willing to further education.

- Candidate D: For: responds to unexpected events adequately; can concentrate very well; solves problems extremely well; takes responsibility seriously. Against: regarded as know‑it‑all; has a hot temper.

## My Position (agent_2)
- My Last Vote: Candidate C
- My Current Working Favorite: Candidate C
- Rationale: Candidate C is reliable, makes quick correct decisions, stays calm, understands tech; despite communication issue, overall fit strong.
- Confidence: 70 %

## Other Agents' Positions
- agent_1: Vote Candidate C (evidence: 100 % reliable)
- agent_3: Vote Candidate B (evidence: handles stress very well)

## Emerging Group View
- Split: C (agents 1 & 2) vs B (agent 3)
- Agreements: Both C and B have critical safety attributes.
- Tensions: Communication issues (C) vs interpersonal remarks (B); other candidates have technical strengths but lack key safety evidence.

## Open Questions / Issues Blocking Consensus
1. How to weight reliability & calmness vs stress tolerance & interpersonal conduct for long‑haul flights?
2. Need any additional data on actual long‑haul experience, safety records, crew feedback.

